{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NQGteAf8dLNsV18hf7MFpt","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"name\": \"position_weighted_switch_impression\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"\", \"hypothesis\": \"People do not weigh every part of a sequence equally: they form their impression of how often the coin switches mainly from one end of the sequence (the last flips they read, or the first), and judge a sequence as more random the closer that position-weighted switch rate comes to a shared ideal switch rate for a random coin. Two sequences with the same overall number of switches can therefore be judged differently depending on whether their switches or their repeats sit at the attended end.\", \"context\": \"experiment1 round 0 candidate 2 lens 2 repair 1\"}\n{\"name\": \"pair_normalized_alternation_contrast\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"\", \"hypothesis\": \"People do not judge each sequence's randomness on an absolute scale; they judge the pair relative to itself. Each person compares how far each sequence's switching rate is from their own ideal switching rate, but the difference between the two is weighed relative to how far both sequences are from that ideal together (divisive contrast normalisation, as in Weber's law): a given gap decides the choice sharply when both sequences are near the ideal and barely matters when both are far from it, so the same sequence is chosen more or less decisively depending on its partner.\", \"context\": \"experiment1 round 2 candidate 0 lens 6\"}\n{\"name\": \"position_weighted_switch_impression\", \"outcome\": \"pruned\", \"detail\": \"312.2 nats behind graded_periodicity_personal_ideal (7.2× dse)\", \"hypothesis\": \"People do not weigh every part of a sequence equally: they form their impression of how often the coin switches mainly from one end of the sequence (the last flips they read, or the first), and judge a sequence as more random the closer that position-weighted switch rate comes to a shared ideal switch rate for a random coin. Two sequences with the same overall number of switches can therefore be judged differently depending on whether their switches or their repeats sit at the attended end.\", \"context\": \"experiment1 end of experiment\"}\n{\"name\": \"pair_normalized_alternation_contrast\", \"outcome\": \"pruned\", \"detail\": \"59.8 nats behind graded_periodicity_personal_ideal (4.6× dse)\", \"hypothesis\": \"People do not judge each sequence's randomness on an absolute scale; they judge the pair relative to itself. Each person compares how far each sequence's switching rate is from their own ideal switching rate, but the difference between the two is weighed relative to how far both sequences are from that ideal together (divisive contrast normalisation, as in Weber's law): a given gap decides the choice sharply when both sequences are near the ideal and barely matters when both are far from it, so the same sequence is chosen more or less decisively depending on its partner.\", \"context\": \"experiment1 end of experiment\"}\n{\"name\": \"run_length_profile_typicality\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"\", \"hypothesis\": \"People judge randomness by the mix of streak lengths a sequence breaks into: each person expects a random coin to produce a characteristic spread of runs — mostly single flips, fewer pairs, rarer triples and only occasionally longer streaks — and a sequence looks random to the extent that the proportions of its runs of length 1, 2, 3 and 4+ match that personal expected mix. Strict alternation (all single runs), uniform blocks like HHTTHHTT (all pairs) and long streaks all have run-length profiles far from the expected mix, and people differ both in the mix they expect and in how strongly a mismatch drives their choice.\", \"context\": \"experiment2 round 2 candidate 1 lens 0\"}\n{\"name\": \"pair_normalized_tally_switch_contrast\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"\", \"hypothesis\": \"People judge the two sequences against each other, not one at a time: each person still compares each sequence's running heads-minus-tails tally span with their own expected span and its switching rate with an ideal switching rate (with a personal left/right lean), but the difference between the two sequences on each cue is weighed relative to how far the pair as a whole sits from the ideal (divisive contrast normalisation). A given gap decides the choice sharply when both sequences are near the ideal and only weakly when both are far from it, so the same sequence is chosen more or less decisively depending on its partner — for instance, between two heavily switching sequences people barely penalise the one that switches more, unlike when it is paired with a moderately switching sequence\n{\"name\": \"run_length_profile_typicality\", \"outcome\": \"pruned\", \"detail\": \"640.6 nats behind tally_span_switch_ideal_periodic_unit (7.9× dse)\", \"hypothesis\": \"People judge randomness by the mix of streak lengths a sequence breaks into: each person expects a random coin to produce a characteristic spread of runs — mostly single flips, fewer pairs, rarer triples and only occasionally longer streaks — and a sequence looks random to the extent that the proportions of its runs of length 1, 2, 3 and 4+ match that personal expected mix. Strict alternation (all single runs), uniform blocks like HHTTHHTT (all pairs) and long streaks all have run-length profiles far from the expected mix, and people differ both in the mix they expect and in how strongly a mismatch drives their choice.\", \"context\": \"experiment2 end of experiment\"}\n{\"name\": \"first_read_anchored_impression\", \"outcome\": \"rejected\", \"detail\": \"too slow to fit: the fit of 'first_read_anchored_impression' (target_accept 0.8) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.\", \"hypothesis\": \"People do not weigh the two sequences' impressions equally: they read the left sequence first, and its impression of randomness anchors the comparison, so the first-read sequence's impression counts by a \n{\"name\": \"ending_switch_recency_rule_built\", \"outcome\": \"rejected\", \"detail\": \"too slow to fit: the fit of 'ending_switch_recency_rule_built' (target_accept 0.8) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refinement of the incumbent `person_switch_ideal_pattern_rule_built` (tally span judged against a personal expected span, switching judged by closeness to a personal ideal switching rate, chunk variet\n{\"name\": \"terminal_streak_recency_pattern_rule\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refinement of the incumbent `person_switch_ideal_pattern_rule_built` (tally span judged against a personal expected span, switching judged by closeness to a personal ideal switching rate, chunk variety and a rule-built penalty scaled by a personal pattern sensitivity, a personal left/right lean). The one change: people weigh the end of a sequence, the flips they read last, more than its interior — a sequence that finishes on a streak (the coin \\\"getting stuck\\\" at the end, HTHHTT, THTHHH) looks less random, and every flip of the final run beyond the first counts against it, by a shared terminal-streak penalty. Of two sequences with the same number of switches, people therefore pick the one that ends with a switch, addressing the critique that among equal-switch pairs people choose the seq\n{\"name\": \"terminal_run_pattern_rule_built\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refinement of `streak_excess_pattern_rule_built` (tally span judged against a personal expected span with person-specific, length-scaled sensitivity; switching judged by closeness to an ideal rate with a person-specific weight; chunk variety and a rule-built penalty scaled by a personal pattern sensitivity; a personal left/right lean; and a shared penalty on streaks). The one change is where the streak is felt: people read a sequence left to right and judge it most by how it ends, so the streak that counts against randomness is the run the sequence finishes on — every repeated flip of its final run (…HH, …TTT) makes it look less random, while a sequence ending on a switch looks most random — replacing the penalty on the longest run anywhere. Of two sequences with equal switch counts, people th","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba583503-b00e-4610-bef9-99cc91309314","uuid":"955ef571-7302-4923-899e-9cc7f9941881","timestamp":"2026-10-04T07:48:56.263Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\"name\": \"position_weighted_switch_impression\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"\", \"hypothesis\": \"People do not weigh every part of a sequence equally: they form their impression of how often the coin switches mainly from one end of the sequence (the last flips they read, or the first), and judge a sequence as more random the closer that position-weighted switch rate comes to a shared ideal switch rate for a random coin. Two sequences